course: everyday
lesson: 280
title: Illusions that fool the eye and adverts that fool the wallet
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Seeing is partly guessing. Your brain judges size and length by what surrounds a thing, so it can be fooled: two equal lines with arrow ends look different (the Müller-Lyer illusion), and the moon looks huge near the horizon although it is about the same size all night. A ruler tells the truth when the eye does not.
explain: Light can play tricks too. A straw in water looks bent because light changes direction as it passes from water to air (refraction). On a hot road, a «puddle» ahead that vanishes is a mirage: hot air bends light from the sky towards your eyes.
explain: Adverts are made to make you want things. They show toys at their very best, sometimes with effects, put sugary cereals at children's eye level, and add «free» toys to meals. Small print like «batteries not included» or «each sold separately» tells the real cost.
explain: Example: a girl in Texas sees a toy drone zooming through a forest on TV. The real one is small, flies for a few minutes and needs batteries. The advert was not exactly a lie, but it showed the toy at its very best.
explain: Prices and offers have tricks too: «up to 70% off» means only a few things are that cheap, and $9.99 is almost exactly $10. Influencers are often paid to praise products, and «free» games earn money from small purchases.
fact: Fortune tellers and horoscopes use general statements that fit almost anyone; there is no evidence they predict the future.
fact: When two lines look different in length, measure them with a ruler, because the brain can be fooled by their surroundings.
fact: A straw in water looks bent because light changes direction as it passes from water to air; this is called refraction.
fact: The moon looks bigger near the horizon only because the brain compares it with trees and buildings.
fact: A mirage on a hot road is hot air bending light from the sky, not real water.
fact: Some pictures, like the duck-rabbit, can be seen in two ways, which shows that seeing is partly guessing.
fact: Professional magicians plan audience help carefully and safely, and you can say no if you don't want to.
fact: Adverts show toys at their best, sometimes with effects; «pictures are for illustration» means the real toy may do less.
fact: Read the small print, like «batteries not included» or «each sold separately», because extra parts cost more.
fact: Sugary cereals with cartoon characters are put on low shelves on purpose, at children's eye level.
fact: A «free» toy with a meal is part of the price and is there to make you want the meal.
fact: «Up to 70% off» means only a few things are that cheap; prices like $9.99 or £4.99 are almost the same as the round number.
fact: Influencers are often paid to praise products; look for #ad, «sponsored» or «paid partnership».
fact: Never buy gems, coins or loot boxes in a game without a parent's permission; small purchases add up fast.
exercise: A horoscope says «This week you will meet someone important». How should you treat it? | As fun if you like, but not a reason for real decisions | As a true message about your future that you must plan around | As a warning you must obey | As proof the stars control people
exercise: Why do fortune tellers' words often seem to fit you? | They use general statements that fit almost anyone | They secretly know everyone in town | They really can see the future in cards, stars and tea leaves | They read your mind through your hands
exercise: Two lines with arrow ends look different lengths. How can you find the truth? | Measure them with a ruler | Look at them for longer | Ask which one looks longer to a friend | Turn the page upside down
exercise: Why does a straw in a glass of water look bent? | Light changes direction as it passes from water to air | The water softens the straw and bends it a little at the top | The glass is curved like a magnifier | Cold water makes plastic bend
exercise: The moon rising over the hills in Colorado looks huge. What is true? | It is an illusion; the moon is about the same size all night | The moon comes much closer to Earth when it is rising over hills | The moon swells when it is near the ground | The air near the ground is a magnifying lens
exercise: On a hot summer road in Arizona, you see «water» ahead that vanishes as you drive closer. What is it? | A mirage: hot air bending light from the sky | A real puddle that dries in seconds in the hot sun | Oil that the sun makes shiny | A reflection of the car's headlights
exercise: A picture can look like a duck or a rabbit. What does it show? | The brain chooses how to see it; seeing is partly guessing | The picture changes when you blink | Only clever people see both | One of the animals is hidden in the paper and shows through
exercise: A magician at a theatre asks you to help on stage. What is true? | It is safe to play along, and you can say no if you want | You must go up, even if you are shy and really don't want to | Magicians choose helpers to trick them meanly | Only adults are allowed on stage
exercise: A TV advert shows a toy car racing up walls and jumping ramps. What should you remember? | Adverts show toys at their best, sometimes with effects | The toy will do exactly the same at home | Adverts are checked so they never exaggerate what a toy can do | The bigger the toy looks, the bigger it is
exercise: A toy box says «batteries not included». Why read that before asking for it? | You will need to buy batteries, which costs extra | It means the toy is broken and the shop is selling it cheaply | It means the toy runs on sunlight | It means the toy is free
exercise: Why are cartoon cereals usually on the low shelves in supermarkets? | So children see them and ask their parents to buy them | Because they are the heaviest boxes | Because tall people don't like them | Because they are the healthiest cereals, so shops put them first
exercise: A fast-food meal comes with a free toy. Why? | To make children want the meal | Because the toy costs nothing to make | Because the restaurant is being kind | So children eat less food
exercise: A sign says «UP TO 70% OFF». What does «up to» mean? | Only a few things are that cheap; most have smaller discounts | Everything in the whole shop is 70% off, including the newest things | Prices will go up by 70% soon | You must buy 70 things to get a discount
exercise: A game costs $9.99. Why do shops choose prices like this? | It looks cheaper than $10, though it is almost the same | It is exactly half the price of $20 | It is the law in the USA | Shops cannot give change for round numbers, so they avoid them
exercise: A YouTuber says she «loves» a new trainer. What should you look for? | #ad, «sponsored» or «paid partnership» | How many times she says she loves it in the video | The colour of the trainers | Whether she is smiling
exercise: A free game keeps offering gems for real money. What is right? | Never buy without a parent's permission | Buy a few, since the game was free | Use a parent's card without asking | Buy as many as you can before the offer ends
test: Your friend's horoscope says Thursday is unlucky, so she wants to skip a match. What is wise? | Play anyway, as horoscopes cannot predict the future | Skip it to be safe | Change her name to stop bad luck | Ask the horoscope for a better day and play on that one instead
test: A pencil in a glass of water seems to break at the water's surface. What is happening? | Light bends as it passes between water and air | The water is pushing the two halves of the pencil apart | The pencil is made of two parts | The glass has a crack in it
test: Which is the best tool to settle an argument about which of two lines is longer? | A ruler | Your eyes from closer up | A vote by the class | A torch shining on them
test: Why does the moon look bigger near the horizon than high in the sky? | The brain compares it with trees and buildings nearby | It really comes much closer to the Earth every evening | Clouds act like a magnifying glass | The moon grows bigger at night
test: A toy box shows a whole castle, but in small print it says «each sold separately». What does that mean? | The other pieces cost extra money | All the pieces in the picture are inside the box | The castle is free with the toy | The box contains two toys
test: A gamer you watch says a headset is amazing, and the video says «paid partnership». What does it tell you? | The company paid him to talk about it | He bought it with his own money | It is the best headset in the world, tested by gamers | The video is fake
test: A child wants a meal only because of the plastic toy. What is really happening? | The toy is part of the price and the selling plan | The restaurant gives the toys away for free, out of kindness | The toy makes the food healthier | The meal is cheaper than usual
test: A shop's poster says «Everything up to 50% off!» and your jumper is only 10% off. Was the poster lying? | No, «up to» means only some items have the biggest cut | Yes, every single item in the shop should be 50% off | Yes, posters must show the smallest discount | No, because jumpers never count
test: You see a mirage of water on a desert road. If you drive there, what will you find? | A dry road, because it was only bent light | A small lake | Puddles of tar that melted in the hot sun and spread out | Wet sand from an old river
test: straw looks snapped in my glass of water, why | Light bends going from water to air, it's refraction | The water really bends the straw | The glass is thicker at the bottom | The straw goes soft in the cold water and bends over
test: road looks wet way ahead but it hasnt rained?? | It's a mirage, hot air bending light from the sky | There's a hidden puddle up ahead | The tar melts and turns to water | Cars leave water behind them as they drive along on hot days
test: ad says batteries not included, so what | You'll have to buy batteries too, it costs more | The toy runs fine without any batteries at all, don't worry | The batteries come in a later post | The shop will give you them free
test: my fave youtuber loves this slime, should i get it | Check for #ad, they're often paid to say they love it | Yes, youtubers only ever show things they really love themselves | Yes, if the video has lots of likes | No, slime is always a scam
explain: A family in Florida bought a toy drone after an exciting advert; at home it flew for a few minutes, crashed and broke, and it was nothing like the advert.
explain: A man paid a fortune teller every week for years because she said bad luck was coming; he lost a lot of money and nothing she said ever came true.
fact: Adverts are made to make people want things, and people often feel disappointed when the real thing arrives.
fact: People lose a lot of money every year to fortune tellers and other tricks that use guesses that fit almost anyone.
test: ad shows the toy car doing loops n flying, its gonna do that? | Probably not, adverts show toys at their best | Yes, adverts have to show exactly what the toy can do at home | Yes, if you buy the batteries | Yes, all toys do that
test: saw water on the road on a hot day but it vanished when we got close | A mirage, hot air bending light from the sky | Real water that dried up | A puddle that the car in front splashed away as it drove | A trick of the car's windscreen
test: lady reckons she can tell my future for 20 quid, worth it? | No, they say general things that fit anyone | Yes, she can really see the future, that's why it costs money | Yes, if she has a crystal ball | Yes, if she knows your star sign
test: did u hear a man in the desert "drove for hours to a lake that wasn't there". real lesson? | A mirage is bent light from the sky, not water | Mirages are real lakes that dry up | Deserts hide water just under the road | Only tired people ever see mirages
test: my mate says a fortune teller "told his aunt exactly when she'd win the lottery and she did". what's likely | It's a rumour; there's no evidence fortune tellers predict anything | It's true, some people can see the future and win prizes with it | Horoscopes are always right in the end | Fortune tellers use real science
test: ppl in class say Ella's mum "spent 5 grand on game coins" and laugh. spread it? | No, it's gossip that could be untrue and hurtful | Yes, it's a funny story | Yes, and ask Ella about it at lunch in front of everyone | Yes, post it in the class chat
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a straw placed in water appears bent. | Light changes direction passing from water to air, called refraction | The water softens the straw and bends it | The glass of the vessel magnifies only one portion of the straw | The eye cannot focus through water
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the phrase «pictures are for illustration» in an advertisement? | The actual product may do less than is shown | The product is guaranteed to match the picture | The picture shows a larger model sold separately | The picture is a photograph of the very item sold
exam: Whereas a price is stated as $9.99, select the response consistent with the established facts. | It is almost exactly the same as ten dollars | It is approximately nine dollars | It is half the price of ten dollars | It includes a further item of a value of one dollar, given free
exam: State the significance of the notation «#ad» or «paid partnership» upon a post by an influencer. | The influencer has been paid to praise the product | The product has been tested by scientists | The product is distributed free of charge | The influencer purchased the product personally, with his own funds
